    "markdown": "---\nkind: topic\ntitle: \"Farkas' Lemma\"\ndescription: \"Either a linear system has a nonnegative solution, or a single vector proves it has none. The theorem behind LP duality.\"\ncategories: [foundations, polyhedra, duality]\norder: 3\nlevel: intro\nprerequisites: \"Cones; separating hyperplanes\"\n---\n\n\n\n## The idea\n\nYou are given some vectors $a_1, \\dots, a_n$ and a target $b$. Can you reach $b$ by adding\n**nonnegative** multiples of the $a_j$? In other words, is $b$ in the cone they generate?\n\nIf yes, the multipliers prove it: anyone can check $b = \\sum_j x_j a_j$ with $x \\ge 0$.\n**Farkas' lemma** says that if no, there is an equally checkable proof of *that*: a\nsingle vector $y$ that makes a non-negative inner product with every $a_j$ but a\nnegative one with $b$. Geometrically, $y$ is the normal of a hyperplane through the\norigin with all the $a_j$ on one side and $b$ on the other.\n\nThe result is due to @farkas1902. It is the separating hyperplane theorem, specialized\nto cones, and everything about LP duality follows from it.\n\n::: {#cell-farkas-ojs-data .cell execution_count=2}\n\n::: {#farkas-ojs-data .cell-output .cell-output-display}\n```{=html}\n<script type=\"ojs-define\">{\"contents\": [{\"name\": \"farkas\", \"value\": [{\"theta\": 0, \"b\": [3.0, 0.0], \"inside\": false, \"p\": [2.4, 1.2], \"y\": [-0.447213595499958, 0.8944271909999159], \"line\": [[4.0, 2.0000000000000004], [-4.0, -2.0000000000000004]], \"ya1\": -0.0, \"ya2\": 2.24, \"yb\": -1.34}, {\"theta\": 5, \"b\": [2.988584094275237, 0.2614672282429745], \"inside\": false, \"p\": [2.495454166717379, 1.2477270833586895], \"y\": [-0.4472135954999582, 0.8944271909999159], \"line\": [[4.0, 2.0000000000000013], [-4.0, -2.0000000000000013]], \"ya1\": -0.0, \"ya2\": 2.24, \"yb\": -1.1}, {\"theta\": 10, \"b\": [2.954423259036624, 0.520944533000791], \"inside\": false, \"p\": [2.5719164204296154, 1.2859582102148077], \"y\": [-0.4472135954999581, 0.8944271909999159]